.TH TATAMI\-MKPKG 8 "" "tatami"
.SH NAME
tatami\-mkpkg \- build a tatami package from a recipe, in a sandbox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tatami\-mkpkg
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tatami\-mkpkg
reads a recipe, the
.BR Tatamifile ,
runs the
.B build()
and
.B package()
functions it defines inside a
.BR bwrap (1)
sandbox, and packs the result into a package for
.BR tatami (8).
.PP
It is a standalone, statically linked C++ program.
It replaces the former bash script: no shell, no
.BR tar ,
no compression program and no configuration file is involved on the host.
The recipe is read by a parser of its own (the host never runs it), sources are
unpacked and the package is written with libarchive, and the only programs it starts are
.BR bwrap ,
.BR curl
and
.BR md5sum
for remote sources, and
.B tatami
for signing.
.PP
The package is named
.IB name _ version [\- release ]_ arch . extension
for example
.BR zlib_1.3.2\-1_x86_64.tar.pkg ,
and is written to the output directory.
.SH THE TATAMIFILE
The recipe uses a small, strict subset of shell syntax.
It is read by
.B tatami\-mkpkg
itself and nothing in it is executed on the host; only the bodies of
.B build()
and
.B package()
are run, and only inside the sandbox.
A line
.B # Description: \fItext\fR
gives the package a description.
.SS Mandatory
.TP
.B name
Letters, digits and
.BR "+ . ~ : \-" ;
it must start with a letter or digit.
An underscore is not allowed: it separates the name and the version in file names.
.TP
.B version
The same characters.
.TP
.B arch
There is no default.
Without it (or with an empty value)
.B tatami\-mkpkg
prints
.B Error: arch is not defined in Tatamifile
and exits with status 1.
Use the target architecture, such as
.BR x86_64 ,
or
.B noarch
for architecture\-independent packages;
.BR noarch ,
.B all
and
.B any
(in any case) mean a package that is not tied to an architecture.
.TP
.B build()
Run in the source directory
.RB ( $SRC )
and must install into
.BR $PKG ,
for example with
.BR "make DESTDIR=$PKG install" .
.SS Optional
.TP
.B release
The package version becomes
.IB version \- release\fR.
Without it the name is
.IB name _ version _ arch\fR.
.TP
.B source
A word or an array of files and URLs; see
.BR SOURCES .
.TP
.B depends
An array of relations, one per element:
.BR libfoo ,
.BR "libbar >= 2.0" ,
.BR "a | b" .
An element may not contain a comma.
.TP
.B package()
Run after
.B build()
in the same sandbox, with the same environment.
Useful to split installing from building.
.SS Ignored
Every other assignment is read to the end of its line or array and discarded, without
being interpreted: in particular
.BR makedepends ,
.BR conflicts ,
.B replaces
and
.BR provides .
They are not parsed, not checked, and do not appear in the package's metadata;
build dependencies are what the masterdir contains.
Any other function than
.B build()
and
.B package()
is skipped too.
.SS Syntax
A statement is
.IB NAME = word\fR,
.IB NAME =( words... )
(an array may span lines and contain comments) or
.IB NAME () " { ... }" .
Quotes
.RB ( ' ' " and " \(dq\(dq ),
backslash escapes and
.B #
comments are understood.
.BR $name ,
.B ${name}
and likewise
.BR version " and " release
are expanded in values; an unset variable is an error.
Command substitution is not supported.
Anything else \- a command, a pipe, a stray character \- is a syntax error that names the line,
and nothing is run.
The body of a function is matched by its braces (quotes and comments are respected)
and handed to the sandbox as written.
.SS Example
.nf
# Description: Compression library
name=zlib
version=1.3.2
release=1
arch=x86_64
source=(https://example.org/zlib\-$version.tar.gz)
depends=(glibc)

build() {
        cd zlib\-$version
        ./configure \-\-prefix=/usr
        make
}

package() {
        cd zlib\-$version
        make DESTDIR=$PKG install
}
.fi
.SH SOURCES
Each element of
.B source
is a file name or a URL; only the last component of the name is used.
A URL (anything containing
.BR :// )
is downloaded next to the recipe with
.B curl
unless the file is already there.
Files are taken from the recipe's directory.
If a file
.B .md5sum
exists next to the recipe, every source needs a line in it and
.BR md5sum (1)
checks them; a mismatch stops the build.
tar and zip archives
.RB ( .tar ,
.BR .tar.gz ,
.BR .tgz ,
.BR .tar.bz2 ,
.BR .tbz2 ,
.BR .tar.xz ,
.BR .txz ,
.BR .tar.zst ,
.BR .tar.lz
and
.BR .zip )
are unpacked with libarchive, and other files copied, into the source directory.
An archive entry with an absolute path or a
.B ..
component is refused.
.SH ISOLATION
The build never touches the host.
The
.B bwrap
command line is fixed in the program; there is no switch to turn the sandbox off, to build
on the host, or to relax it.
.IP \(bu 3
The root file system is the
.BR masterdir ,
mounted read\-only
.RB ( "\-\-ro\-bind " \fImasterdir\fR " /" ).
It is a directory with a base system \- at least
.B /bin/sh
and a toolchain \- that you prepare (see
.B make bootstrap\-env
in the source tree for the empty layout); the build runs with exactly what is in it, and
cannot change it.
.B bwrap
cannot create mount points on a read\-only root, so
.BR dev ,
.BR proc ,
.B tmp
and
.B build
are created in the masterdir if they are missing.
.IP \(bu 3
.BR /dev ,
.B /proc
and a tmpfs
.B /tmp
are mounted fresh.
.IP \(bu 3
The workspace is bound read\-write at
.BR /build :
.B /build/src
holds the unpacked sources and is the working directory, and
.B /build/pkg
is the package root.
They are separate directories, so the sources can never be mistaken for the package.
.IP \(bu 3
All namespaces are unshared
.RB ( \-\-unshare\-all :
no network), the sandbox dies with its parent and runs in a new session.
.IP \(bu 3
The environment is cleared
.RB ( \-\-clearenv )
and set explicitly:
.BR PATH ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR SRC=/build/src ,
.BR PKG=/build/pkg ,
.BR DESTDIR=/build/pkg ,
and
.BR name ,
.B version
and
.BR release .
.IP \(bu 3
The script is run by
.BR "/bin/sh \-c" ,
with
.B set \-e
so that a failing command ends the build: the two functions as written in the recipe, then
.B build
and, if defined,
.BR package .
.PP
If
.B bwrap
cannot be started the build fails with an error that names bubblewrap; it is never run
unprotected.
Because the sandbox reads the masterdir, a build can only use what the masterdir contains.
.SH WORKSPACE
The workspace is a directory
.BI /tmp/ hash
on the host, where
.I hash
is 24 random lowercase letters and digits read from
.BR /dev/urandom .
It is created with
.BR mkdir (2)
(mode 0700), so a name that exists is never reused; the sources are put into its
.B src
directory and it holds the
.B pkg
directory that becomes the package.
.PP
It is owned by a C++ object whose destructor deletes it recursively \- read\-only
directories made by the build included \- on every way out of the program: a finished
build, a failed one, an error and an exception, and SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP (which are
passed on to the sandbox first).
The deletion is refused for anything but a path of the form
.BI /tmp/ hash
(16 to 32 lowercase letters and digits).
Only SIGKILL or a crash of the machine can leave it behind.
.SH PACKAGE
After the build the contents of
.B /build/pkg
must not be empty.
The package is written with libarchive: the metadata
.B .META
first, then the tree, sorted, with numeric owners and hard links preserved.
.B .META
holds
.BR name ,
.BR version ,
.BR arch ,
the description and the
.BR depends .
The extension and the compression are fixed when tatami is built
.RB ( PKG_EXT ,
by default
.BR tar.pkg ;
.BR PKG_COMPRESSION ,
by default
.BR zst ):
the compression is the
.B \-z
option, then the one the extension ends in
.RB ( gz , xz , zst , bz2 , lz4 , lzma ),
then the build\-time default.
With an empty
.B PKG_EXT
the extension is
.BI tar. compression
.RB ( tar
for
.BR none ).
Neither can be set in a configuration file or the environment.
.SH INSTALL SCRIPTS
If a file named
.B tatami.install
exists in the same directory as the
.BR Tatamifile ,
it is bundled automatically into the root of the package as the hidden file
.BR .INSTALL ;
nothing needs to be set in the recipe.
It may define two bash functions:
.TP
.B pre_install
Run by
.BR tatami (8)
before the package's files are extracted. If it exits non\-zero the whole
transaction is rolled back.
.TP
.B post_install
Run after the transaction was committed. A failure is only reported.
.PP
Both receive the package version as their argument and find the installation root in
.BR TATAMI_ROOT .
There are no scripts for removal.
.B .INSTALL
is not installed as a file.
.SH SIGNING
If a signing key exists, the build signs its results as part of the build.
The key is the file named by the environment variable
.B TATAMI_PRIVKEY
(which must exist, otherwise the build stops with
.BR "signing key \fIfile\fP (TATAMI_PRIVKEY) not found" ),
or else
.BR ~/.tatami/privkey ;
it is made with
.BR "tatami keygen" .
With a key,
.B tatami\-mkpkg
needs the
.B tatami
binary and checks for it before building.
Right after the package is written, by running that binary, it
.IP \(bu 3
refreshes the index
.B repo.solv
of the output directory, which records the SHA\-256 checksum of every package in it
(the directory is listed in a repository list in a throw\-away root below
.BR $TMPDIR ,
or
.BR /tmp ,
that is removed afterwards),
.IP \(bu 3
signs the package, writing
.IB package .sig,
.IP \(bu 3
signs the index, writing
.BR repo.solv.sig .
.PP
Publish all of these files together; clients put the public key (the
.B .pub
file made by
.BR "tatami keygen" )
into
.B /etc/tatami/keys/
(see
.BR tatami.conf (5)).
Without a key the package is built unsigned, the output directory is not indexed and
.B Not signing
is printed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " \fIrecipe\fP
Recipe to build (default
.BR Tatamifile ).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIdir\fP
Where to put the package (default: the current directory).
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-masterdir " " \fIdir\fP
The root file system of the sandbox
.RB (default:
.BR /var/lib/tatami/masterdir ).
It must exist.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-compression " " \fIc\fP
One of
.BR gz ,
.BR zst ,
.BR xz ,
.BR bz2 ,
.BR lz4 ,
.B lzma
or
.BR none ,
for this build only.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sign
Do not sign, even if a key exists.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help and exit.
.PP
The options of the former script
.RB ( \-c ,
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-\-no\-bulkhead ,
.BR \-\-no\-sandbox ,
.BR \-\-bulkhead )
are gone, and the configuration file is not read.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TATAMI
The tatami binary used for signing and indexing (default: found in
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.B TATAMI_PRIVKEY
The signing key.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where the throw\-away root for the index is made (default
.BR /tmp ).
.PP
No other variable of the host reaches the build.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B Tatamifile
The recipe.
.TP
.B tatami.install
Optional install script, bundled as
.BR .INSTALL .
.TP
.B .md5sum
Optional checksums of the sources.
.TP
.B /var/lib/tatami/masterdir
The default masterdir.
.TP
.B /tmp/\fIhash\fR
The workspace of a build; deleted afterwards.
.TP
.B ~/.tatami/privkey
The default signing key.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
if the package was built, non\-zero otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tatami (8),
.BR tatami.conf (5),
.BR bwrap (1)
